# The first program in this tree that prints a number: an unsigned decimal
# printer built on `/ 10` and `residuum 10` (spec §5.4, settled 2026-09-25).
# numerus_decimalis.exsc's header lists what each printed number is there to
# catch; 1-21 name the first line whose BYTE COUNT was wrong, 0 is all right.
#
# TWO INDEPENDENT ASSERTIONS, and both have to hold:
#   the BYTES   expected.out, compared by check_run with `cmp`
#   the LENGTHS the exit status -- every `scribe_lineam` call's return is
#               checked against the number of digits that number has, so a
#               stray leading zero or a dropped digit fails as a status and
#               not only as a diff. A golden alone would pass a printer that
#               wrote the right characters while miscounting them, and a
#               status alone would pass one that counted right and emitted
#               the wrong digits.
#
# HOW expected.out WAS PRODUCED -- not by this program, which is the point of
# a golden. Python, whose `//` and `%` are FLOOR division, which coincides
# with §5.4's truncation toward zero for these operands because every one of
# them is non-negative (the two differ only at mixed signs; the signed cases
# are pinned at the IR by tests/ir/rem_sign.ir and divrem.ir, and in the
# checker by tests/unit/chk_ty_intdiv.asm):
#
#   python3 -c "
#   vals=[0,7,9,10,11,99,100,255,256,65535,65536,4294967295,4294967296,
#         9223372036854775807,9223372036854775808,18446744073709551615,
#         1000000//7,1000000%7,1000000//7,1000000%7]
#   import sys
#   sys.stdout.write(''.join('%d\n'%v for v in vals))
#   " > tests/programs/numerus_decimalis/expected.out
#
# 140 bytes, 20 lines. The last four are 1000000/7 and 1000000%7 twice: once
# at `u64` and once at `mensura`, which is the pair `cross=yes` below exists
# for.
#
# cross=yes IS LOAD-BEARING HERE, not extra coverage. `mensura` is 64 bits on
# x86_64-linux and 32 on mips64-none-o64 (ADR 0015), so the program's two
# `mensura` lines emit `exsi_div_u(v139, v140, 32)` / `exsi_rem_u(..., 32)` in
# the cross unit where the host unit emits `..., 64` -- a different call at a
# different width from the same source line, running big-endian -- and both
# must print the same six digits and the same one digit. Verified by reading
# the two emitted units: the `, 32)` call appears in the
# `--hospes mips64-none-o64` unit and in no other.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-26 (reference backend, then the differential and cross
# phases driven by hand, one program at a time -- tests/run.sh was not run):
#   reference   exit 0, 140 bytes, byte-identical to expected.out
#   gcc   -O0   exit 0, 140 bytes, identical    (UBSan on, -fno-sanitize-recover)
#   gcc   -O2   exit 0, 140 bytes, identical
#   clang -O0   exit 0, 140 bytes, identical
#   clang -O2   exit 0, 140 bytes, identical
#   cross       clang --target=mips64-unknown-linux-musl -mabi=n32, run under
#               qemu-mipsn32: exit 0, 140 bytes, identical. readelf confirms
#               "2's complement, big endian".
#
# No stdin=: the program reads nothing. It draws `ambitus` only (spec §4.6),
# so its syscall set is write and exit_group.
TEST: expect-exit=0 cross=yes
